1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.710
[S01] The most effective thinking curriculum in the history of Western civilization is not

2
00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:09.439
[S01] taught in any MBA program, any law school, or any leadership course.

3
00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:15.710
[S01] Instead, it's a book written 2,300 years ago called Euclid's Elements, and it has more documented

4
00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:21.109
[S01] cases of reshaping how great minds think than Oxford and Cambridge combined.

5
00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:25.609
[S01] Over my more than 13 years as a peer-reviewed scholar and educator, I've come to believe

6
00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:30.440
[S01] that Euclid's Elements is the single most under-leveraged intellectual resource available

7
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.469
[S01] to anyone alive today.

8
00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:36.820
[S01] And in this video, I'm going to showcase four great thinkers who read this book, the

9
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.890
[S01] concrete impact it had on them, and show you how it can permanently transform how you

10
00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:42.890
[S01] think too.

11
00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:47.960
[S01] So let's start with our first thinker, Abraham Lincoln, because his story is the most concrete

12
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.920
[S01] illustration of why someone would pick up this geometry textbook and carry it in a

13
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.640
[S01] saddlebag across Illinois.

14
00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.210
[S01] You see, Lincoln had almost no formal education, a few months of scattered schooling as a

15
00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.729
[S01] child, and then nothing.

16
00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:06.109
[S01] In fact, he actually taught himself law by reading borrowed books at night.

17
00:01:06.109 --> 00:01:09.700
[S01] But at some point in his early legal career, he ran into a problem.

18
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.500
[S01] He was losing arguments he should have been winning, and it wasn't because the facts

19
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.920
[S01] were against him, but because he didn't know what it meant to actually prove something.

20
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.780
[S01] He later described this moment of realization.

21
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.349
[S01] "You can never make a lawyer," he stated, "if you do not understand what demonstrate

22
00:01:26.349 --> 00:01:27.349
[S01] means."

23
00:01:27.349 --> 00:01:31.209
[S01] And so he went home to his father's farm, and he stayed there until he could recite

24
00:01:31.209 --> 00:01:35.209
[S01] any proposition in the first six books of Euclid from memory.

25
00:01:35.209 --> 00:01:37.219
[S01] Then he went back to his law practice.

26
00:01:37.219 --> 00:01:41.310
[S01] Now, Euclid's Elements is a geometry book, not a law textbook.

27
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:44.890
[S01] And so what was Euclid actually training Lincoln to do?

28
00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:48.569
[S01] Well, to answer it, we need to spend a moment with the book itself.

29
00:01:48.569 --> 00:01:52.849
[S01] You see, the Elements opens not with a theorem, but with definitions.

30
00:01:52.849 --> 00:01:55.140
[S01] "A point is that which has no part.

31
00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.310
[S01] A line is a breadthless length.

32
00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.969
[S01] A straight line lies evenly with the points on itself."

33
00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:07.030
[S01] In fact, Euclid defines 23 terms before he begins trying to prove a single thing, and

34
00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.219
[S01] then he does something even more important.

35
00:02:09.219 --> 00:02:14.719
[S01] He states what are called postulates, which you can think of as his assumptions explicitly.

36
00:02:14.719 --> 00:02:19.310
[S01] Postulate 1: A straight line can be drawn from any point to any other point.

37
00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:23.310
[S01] Postulate 2: A finite straight line can be extended indefinitely.

38
00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.759
[S01] And then he states what he calls his common notions, which are essentially basic logical

39
00:02:27.759 --> 00:02:30.340
[S01] principles he'll rely on throughout the text.

40
00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:35.939
[S01] Common Notion 1: Things which are equal to the same thing are also equal to one another.

41
00:02:35.939 --> 00:02:38.879
[S01] And so what makes this all so remarkable?

42
00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:44.259
[S01] Well, you see, every time Euclid moves from one step in a proof to the next, he cites

43
00:02:44.259 --> 00:02:46.509
[S01] the rule that justifies the move.

44
00:02:46.509 --> 00:02:51.949
[S01] In other words, he doesn't just state something like "therefore," but instead something like

45
00:02:51.949 --> 00:02:56.870
[S01] "therefore by common notion 1," or "therefore by proposition 4."

46
00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:00.810
[S01] In other words, nothing in Euclid's Elements is simply asserted.

47
00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.229
[S01] Instead, everything is demonstrated and cited.

48
00:03:04.229 --> 00:03:08.539
[S01] And so what Euclid trained Lincoln to do, and what he trains you to do if you read

49
00:03:08.539 --> 00:03:14.180
[S01] him, is to refuse to accept any step in an argument that isn't justified by an explicit

50
00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.180
[S01] rule.

51
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.900
[S01] You see, most people argue by assertion only.

52
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:23.120
[S01] They say things like "clearly," or "obviously," or it follows that and just move on.

53
00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.370
[S01] But Euclid makes that impossible.

54
00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:27.229
[S01] Every gap has to be closed.

55
00:03:27.229 --> 00:03:29.840
[S01] Every conclusion has to be earned.

56
00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.509
[S01] And so here's an exercise you can engage in from Euclid that is similar to what Lincoln

57
00:03:34.509 --> 00:03:35.509
[S01] did.

58
00:03:35.509 --> 00:03:39.509
[S01] The next time you make an argument, in a negotiation, a meeting, a piece of writing,

59
00:03:39.509 --> 00:03:42.729
[S01] whatever, force yourself to write it in three layers.

60
00:03:42.729 --> 00:03:45.400
[S01] First, define your key terms precisely.

61
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:50.620
[S01] What exactly do you mean by success, efficiency, fairness, even risk?

62
00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:55.949
[S01] Second, state your assumptions explicitly, the things you're taking as given or fundamental

63
00:03:55.949 --> 00:03:57.060
[S01] without proving them.

64
00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:03.180
[S01] Third, for every move you make from one claim to the next, name the rule you're applying.

65
00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:08.229
[S01] If you can't name it, you see, your thinking and understanding probably isn't all that

66
00:04:08.229 --> 00:04:09.229
[S01] clear.

67
00:04:09.229 --> 00:04:13.620
[S01] And so Lincoln went back to his law practice, understanding this kind of discipline.

68
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.259
[S01] And it's not a coincidence that he became one of the greatest legal minds in American

69
00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:18.259
[S01] history.

70
00:04:18.259 --> 00:04:22.629
[S01] But whereas Lincoln came to Euclid through professional necessity, our second thinker

71
00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:25.699
[S01] here, Thomas Hobbes, came to him by accident.

72
00:04:25.699 --> 00:04:29.470
[S01] And the story of that encounter is one of the most dramatic intellectual conversion

73
00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:30.769
[S01] stories on record.

74
00:04:30.769 --> 00:04:36.550
[S01] You see, Hobbes was 40 years old, already an educated man, already a scholar by any

75
00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:41.110
[S01] standard, when he wandered into a library and came across Euclid's Elements, open

76
00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:46.439
[S01] to Book 1, Proposition 47, the famous Pythagorean theorem.

77
00:04:46.439 --> 00:04:48.329
[S01] From there, he read the conclusion.

78
00:04:48.329 --> 00:04:53.329
[S01] "In a right-angled triangle, the square on the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares

79
00:04:53.329 --> 00:04:54.750
[S01] on the other two sides."

80
00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.560
[S01] His immediate reaction reportedly was, "By God, this is impossible."

81
00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.120
[S01] So he read the proof.

82
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:06.810
[S01] The proof cited earlier propositions, so he read those, which cited earlier ones still.

83
00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:11.199
[S01] And he followed the chain all the way back to the definitions and postulates at the

84
00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:15.180
[S01] beginning of Book 1, and realized that everything was airtight.

85
00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.750
[S01] He couldn't find a single gap.

86
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.870
[S01] The conclusion was not only true, but necessarily true, given the axioms.

87
00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:25.019
[S01] And Hobbes walked out of that library a changed thinker.

88
00:05:25.019 --> 00:05:30.910
[S01] In fact, his political philosophy, the Leviathan, is structured as a quasi-Euclidean argument.

89
00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:36.310
[S01] Definitions first, then axioms about human nature, then derived conclusions about what

90
00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:39.149
[S01] political arrangements necessarily follow from those.

91
00:05:39.149 --> 00:05:43.279
[S01] In fact, what Hobbes had stumbled into was Euclid's most powerful technique, what is

92
00:05:43.279 --> 00:05:47.120
[S01] known in formal logic as the reductio ad absurdum.

93
00:06:00.029 --> 00:06:02.790
[S02] Not equal, then one would be larger.

94
00:06:02.819 --> 00:06:06.779
[S02] You could then construct a smaller triangle inside the original one.

95
00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:11.040
[S02] But that smaller triangle would have the same angle sum as the larger one,

96
00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:13.800
[S02] which contradicts results already proven.

97
00:06:13.829 --> 00:06:16.170
[S02] Therefore, the sides must be equal.

98
00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:19.980
[S02] In effect, the assumption of the opposite has destroyed itself.

99
00:06:20.009 --> 00:06:22.439
[S02] And in Book Nine, Proposition Twenty,

100
00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:27.149
[S02] Euclid uses the same technique to prove something that still stops people cold.

101
00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.930
[S02] Namely, the proposition, there are infinitely many prime numbers.

102
00:06:30.959 --> 00:06:33.420
[S02] Again, Euclid assumes the opposite.

103
00:06:33.449 --> 00:06:35.759
[S02] Suppose there are finitely many.

104
00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.079
[S02] Well, list them all.

105
00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:40.379
[S02] Now multiply them all together and add one.

106
00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.839
[S02] Well, this new number is either prime,

107
00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.689
[S02] and it's not on your list, contradicting the assumption,

108
00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:50.670
[S02] or it has a prime factor not on your list, again, contradicting the assumption.

109
00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:54.120
[S02] Either way, the assumption of finitude becomes impossible.

110
00:06:54.149 --> 00:06:58.230
[S02] And so what reductio ad absurdum trains is something powerful.

111
00:06:58.259 --> 00:07:00.269
[S02] When you can't prove something directly,

112
00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:04.680
[S02] simply assume it's false and then follow that assumption wherever it leads.

113
00:07:04.709 --> 00:07:08.370
[S02] If it leads somewhere impossible, well, you've proven your case.

114
00:07:08.399 --> 00:07:10.259
[S02] And this doesn't just apply to mathematics.

115
00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:12.629
[S02] It lies at the heart of effective legal arguments,

116
00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.990
[S02] scientific falsification, and even strategic thinking.

117
00:07:16.019 --> 00:07:19.410
[S02] And so here's an exercise you can engage in with respect to this skill.

118
00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:21.660
[S02] The next time you believe something is true,

119
00:07:21.689 --> 00:07:23.459
[S02] but you struggle to demonstrate it,

120
00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:27.360
[S02] write down its negation, or the opposite claim, at the top of a page.

121
00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:31.139
[S02] Then follow that opposite claim as far as it will take you.

122
00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:34.439
[S02] What would have to be true if the opposite were true?

123
00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.029
[S02] And what would that imply?

124
00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:37.889
[S02] And what would that then imply?

125
00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:41.279
[S02] Keep following the chain until you either reach a contradiction,

126
00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:43.949
[S02] something that violates what you already know,

127
00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:47.970
[S02] or until you realize your original belief was shakier than you thought.

128
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.040
[S02] Both outcomes are valuable.

129
00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:52.079
[S02] The contradiction gives you a proof,

130
00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:55.319
[S02] and the failure to find one tells you something important

131
00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.509
[S02] about the limits of what you actually know.

132
00:07:57.540 --> 00:07:59.670
[S02] And by the way, if you want to work with me personally

133
00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:02.430
[S02] on this skill and so many other critical thinking skills

134
00:08:02.459 --> 00:08:04.740
[S02] that affect the real outcomes in your life,

135
00:08:04.769 --> 00:08:07.620
[S02] then feel free to check out my Critical Thinking Academy

136
00:08:07.649 --> 00:08:09.720
[S02] at the link in the pinned comment below.

137
00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:12.509
[S02] But now that brings us to the third thinker on our list,

138
00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.600
[S02] who read Euclid's Elements, Albert Einstein.

139
00:08:15.629 --> 00:08:17.610
[S02] Now Einstein's encounter with Euclid

140
00:08:17.629 --> 00:08:21.120
[S02] was different from both Lincoln's and Hobbes,

141
00:08:21.149 --> 00:08:23.339
[S02] and what it gave him was different, too.

142
00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:27.329
[S02] Einstein was 12 years old when he received what he later called

143
00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.220
[S02] a holy little geometry book, a copy of Euclid's Elements.

144
00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:33.870
[S02] And what struck him was not any individual proof,

145
00:08:33.899 --> 00:08:35.970
[S02] but something about the method as a whole.

146
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.529
[S02] Here was a system, he thought,

147
00:08:37.559 --> 00:08:41.340
[S02] where you could start with a handful of simple self-evident postulates

148
00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:45.899
[S02] and derive with complete certainty from pure thought alone,

149
00:08:45.929 --> 00:08:49.470
[S02] results that were not at all obvious, the Pythagorean theorem.

150
00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:53.039
[S02] Book One, Proposition Forty Seven, is again a great example.

151
00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:56.879
[S02] You see, Euclid doesn't discover it in one move.

152
00:08:56.909 --> 00:08:57.779
[S02] He builds it.

153
00:08:57.809 --> 00:09:01.019
[S02] Proposition Forty Seven depends on Proposition Forty One,

154
00:09:01.049 --> 00:09:03.750
[S02] which depends on Proposition Thirty Seven,

155
00:09:03.779 --> 00:09:05.940
[S02] which depends on Proposition Thirty Five,

156
00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:08.850
[S02] which depends on Proposition Fourteen, and so on,

157
00:09:08.879 --> 00:09:11.009
[S02] all the way back to the first definitions.

158
00:09:11.039 --> 00:09:13.710
[S02] The entire structure is a dependency chain.

159
00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:16.769
[S02] No step is possible without the steps that precede it.

160
00:09:16.799 --> 00:09:19.740
[S02] And the conclusion, that in any right triangle,

161
00:09:19.769 --> 00:09:22.470
[S02] the areas of the squares on the two shorter sides

162
00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:25.289
[S02] add up to the area of the square on the longest,

163
00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.350
[S02] emerges from this chain with a kind of inevitability

164
00:09:28.379 --> 00:09:31.620
[S02] that feels law-like, almost in a physical sense.

165
00:09:31.649 --> 00:09:35.370
[S02] And so Einstein carried this model of thought into physics.

166
00:09:35.399 --> 00:09:39.120
[S02] For instance, his special relativity begins with two postulates,

167
00:09:39.149 --> 00:09:42.659
[S02] that the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames,

168
00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:46.139
[S02] and that the speed of light is constant for all observers,

169
00:09:46.169 --> 00:09:48.269
[S02] and derives everything else from them.

170
00:09:48.299 --> 00:09:50.429
[S02] That method itself is Euclidean.

171
00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.950
[S02] Start simple, be explicit, build carefully,

172
00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.710
[S02] and let the complexity emerge from the structure.

173
00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:57.629
[S02] And so here's an exercise you can engage in,

174
00:09:57.659 --> 00:10:00.179
[S02] inspired by Einstein's reading of Euclid.

175
00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:02.279
[S02] The next time you face a complex problem,

176
00:10:02.309 --> 00:10:05.100
[S02] don't try to solve it in one move.

177
00:10:05.129 --> 00:10:09.120
[S02] Instead, ask what smaller problems you would need to solve first

178
00:10:09.149 --> 00:10:11.879
[S02] in order for the larger solution to be possible.

179
00:10:11.909 --> 00:10:14.909
[S02] Build the dependency chain backwards.

180
00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:17.190
[S02] What must be true for X to work?

181
00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:19.440
[S02] And what must be true for that to work?

182
00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:21.179
[S02] Keep going until you reach claims

183
00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:23.580
[S02] simple enough to verify directly.

184
00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:25.639
[S02] Then build forward from there.

185
00:10:25.669 --> 00:10:28.769
[S02] And do you see how this isn't just a mathematical habit?

186
00:10:28.799 --> 00:10:33.179
[S02] In fact, it's the architecture of every sustained complex achievement.

187
00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:37.440
[S02] Finally, though, we arrive at the fourth and final thinker here, Bertrand Russell.

188
00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.690
[S02] Russell encountered Euclid at 11 years old

189
00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:41.820
[S02] and later described the experience as

190
00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:45.659
[S02] "one of the great events of my life, as dazzling as first love."

191
00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:48.059
[S02] You see, Russell had been told by his older brother

192
00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:50.879
[S02] that they were gonna go study geometry together.

193
00:10:50.909 --> 00:10:52.710
[S02] He was disappointed, though, to be told

194
00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:55.769
[S02] that he would have to accept the axioms as given,

195
00:10:55.799 --> 00:10:59.700
[S02] that Euclid's postulates couldn't be proved, only assumed.

196
00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:04.830
[S03] ...arrangement, considering each case and showing that it leads to contradiction.

197
00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:09.519
[S03] This is what informal logic is called proof by cases.

198
00:12:09.549 --> 00:12:12.639
[S03] Instead of asserting something like, here's why X is true,

199
00:12:12.669 --> 00:12:16.320
[S03] you assert, here's why everything other than X is impossible.

200
00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:21.179
[S03] And it's one of the most powerful and least taught reasoning techniques in existence.

201
00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:25.820
[S03] So if we step back and take a look at Lincoln, Hobbes, Einstein, and Russell,

202
00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:28.059
[S03] what did Euclid actually give them?

203
00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:30.179
[S03] Because it wasn't really geometry.

204
00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:32.940
[S03] I mean, none of them actually went on to become geometers.

205
00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:39.179
[S03] Instead, what Euclid gave them was a model of what rigorous thinking looks like when it's done completely.

206
00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:41.059
[S03] One, define your terms.

207
00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:42.820
[S03] Two, state your assumptions.

208
00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:45.179
[S03] Three, construct rather than assert.

209
00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:47.340
[S03] Four, cite the rule at every step.

210
00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:51.179
[S03] Five, break complex problems into simpler ones and solve those first.

211
00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:53.740
[S03] And six, when you can't prove something directly,

212
00:12:53.769 --> 00:12:57.340
[S03] assume the opposite and follow it until it destroys itself.

213
00:12:57.370 --> 00:12:59.940
[S03] And these are far more than simply mathematical skills.

214
00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:02.659
[S03] Instead, they're cognitive habits.

215
00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:04.639
